we've been hearing about apple's plans  to launch a completely portless iphone  for a couple of years now but i haven't  really seen anyone go really deep into  explaining all of the reasons why apple  might want to do this and how they'll  actually be able to pull it off for a  wide range of people without causing a  bunch of issues so in this video i'm not  only going to answer those questions but  i'm going to go in depth with technical  explanations on how the different  possible solutions will actually  transfer data without requiring a port  so with that said the first thing we've  got to do is quickly run through all the  portless iphone rumors we've had over  the years before i blow your mind with  some apple patent filings way back in  december of 2019 we got our first rumors  of a portless iphone from ming chi quo  reporting that we should expect at least  one iphone model in 2021 to have no port  at all which of course didn't happen and  then last year quo cooled off on those  rumors saying that if the iphone  abandons lightning in the future it may  directly adopt the portless design with  mag safe support instead of a usb c port  however he also said that right now the  mac safe ecosystem is not mature enough  so the iphone will continue to use the  lightning port for the foreseeable  future now i don't know if i agree about  that because we did get some rumors of  apple switching to usb-c with their  iphone 14 pro lineup but i do agree that  apple will be using the mag safe puck to  make the iphone go portless and in just  a minute i'll explain how the technology  required for transferring data from a  portless iphone to a computer is gonna  work but first we gotta go through the  reasons for why apple would want a  portless iphone in the first place  according to mark german who spoke to  mkbhd on his podcast back in december of  2020 one benefit to going portless is  better water resistance which makes  sense because the lightning port has a  bunch of metal contacts inside of it as  well as potential cracks that can allow  water to seep through if there's enough  water pressure not to mention salt water  which is the worst danger for the iphone  so by removing the port the iphone  should handle water pressure much better  and it may even allow apple to make the  iphone salt water resistant just like  the apple watch already is especially  since we already know that apple is  planning on removing the physical sim  card tray according to dylan dkt on  twitter now as for some other reasons  mark said that removing the port would  give apple more internal iphone space to  work with which could lead to an even  bigger battery which is always nice but  other than that it would hands down  encourage more people to buy airpods  since they would no longer have the  option of using the lightning port for  wired headphones and then finally  removing the port would greatly speed up  the growth of the magsafe ecosystem  since everyone will be forced to use it  as the only means of charging  and trust me once you try maxsafe you'll  want to buy more mac safe accessories  like my all-time favorite magsafe car  vent charger that i love so with all  that said going portless is a  win-win-win situation for apple on every  single front but now it's time to get  into the topic of how apple will pull  off data transfer from the portless  iphone to the mac safe puck and then  down through the usbc cable into a mac  or a pc and it all starts with a single  patent that apple filed for back in  2017  showing off a wireless charging system  that uses magnets to align a puck that  will wirelessly charge another device  using inductive charging coils and if  that sounds familiar it is because it's  actually the magsafe puck that we  already have so believe it or not apple  filed for this patent back in 2017  and the mind-blowing thing about it is  that they originally planned for it to  handle data transfers as well in this  patent image apple shows an external  charging and data puck magnetically  attaching to the back of an iphone which  proves that it's mac safe but then there  are two optical data transfer modules  one built into the center of the puck  and one built into the iphone so that  data will flow into the puck and down  the cable to a computer like it  literally says in this drawing so it's  crazy to think that the magsafe puck was  originally designed with data transfer  in mind and if we take a look at a  teardown of the magsafe puck there is  clearly enough room to put in this  so-called optical data transfer module  but apple obviously held off on this  part because it won't be needed until  apple reveals the portless iphone but if  you're wondering how apple plans to  transfer data using the 2nd gen mac safe  puck apple explains it very clearly in  their patent filing they'll be  transferring data by shining light  through a puck optical window emitted at  various frequencies to send and or  receive data to a device such as an  iphone and if you're wondering what a  puck optical window is it's a special  material on the inside of the puck that  a user cannot see through at all  but optical signals could still transmit  through and more specifically they said  that this material could be fully  transparent to infrared or ultraviolet  light while being reflective to regular  visual light and what exactly is that  optical window well if you take a look  at the max save puck that thin off-white  material is actually what apple was  talking about all along in the patent  you can't see through it but data can  travel through it in the form of  infrared or ultraviolet light so with  all that said the way that the portless  iphone could work with data transfer is  that it's going to come with an optical  data transfer module inside of it which  will then shine uv or ir light in the  form of an optical data signal into a  matching module within a second gen  magsafe puck which would then send the  data to the computer through the usbc  cable at blazing fast speeds and i know  it's going to be blazing fast for sure  because back in 2017 researchers at  eindhoven university of technology  achieved a transfer speed of  42.8 gigabits per second using infrared  light to transmit data yes that's faster  than the 40 gigabit per second  thunderbolt 4 standard that we have  today on apple's max and the m1 ipad pro  which is essentially 80 times faster  than the transfer speed of the current  lightning port on the iphone 13 pro max  this would essentially mean full iphone  to mac backups within a couple of  minutes or transferring around 150  gigabytes of pro res video files in  under a minute yes under a minute but  with all that said i know a lot of you  out there are probably skeptical about  this technology actually working  properly for various different reasons  so now let me go through some of those  common concerns first of all one  potential problem is that the iphone  comes with a painted back underneath the  glass which could mess with the optical  data connection however infrared light  actually passes through paint and that's  why infrared is often used to quote see  through paint layers that are  impenetrable to the human eye so i ended  up looking online for some real world  examples and i found a forum post on  avforums.com  where a user buried his sky magic eye ir  or infrared receiver into the surface of  the wall in his kitchen and he wanted to  paint it so that it didn't stand out so  after two layers of paint over the ir  receiver he discovered that it still  worked perfectly fine to transmit data  to his tv system and that was way back  in 2006 so the paint on the back of the  iphone should not cause any problems for  infrared light data transfer but as for  some of the other problems you  unfortunately wouldn't be able to use an  iphone case while transferring data with  the mac save puck so you'd have to take  it off every single time you want to use  it to transfer data now this is probably  not that big of a deal because airdrop  is by far the most common method to  transfer files and photos but if you're  trying to transfer very large files to a  mac or pc using the cable method you're  gonna have to take the case off to use  this system and in my honest opinion i  feel like this could be a realistic  option for transferring data through a  cable on apple's future portless iphone  especially since apple also created a  system for the optical data transfer  modules within the iphone and mac safe  puck to automatically align with each  other just in case it's not perfectly  aligned and if you think all of this is  just way too crazy there are actually a  couple more options that apple could use  instead if it turns out that this tech  doesn't work as well as apple originally  thought it would like for example they  could pack in a little u1 ultra wideband  chip inside of the max 8 puck and then  have the data converted into a signal  that could pass through the other usbc  end and this would be fairly easy since  the iphone has already packed a u1 chip  ever since the iphone 11. or another  option would be to use the brand new  short range 60.5 gigahertz wireless data  transfer feature that apple secretly  built into the apple watch series 7. so  they would just have to build that tech  into both the portless iphone and the  max a puck to make it work and according  to a mac rumors commenter this  technology would support between 10 and  40 gigabits per second speeds over the  60 gigahertz frequency so it could  easily replace wired connections if it  ends up being reliable enough and the  benefit to using this over the whole  infrared idea is that you won't have to  take your case off at all so that could  actually be a more reasonable choice for  apple since it's more convenient for  users but in either case we know that  apple has some very promising technology  that is able to finally give the iphone  insanely fast 40 gigabits per second  transfer speeds through the mac safe  puck and that right there is what will  make users actually want to upgrade to a  portless iphone i mean i can easily see  apple walk out on stage and announce  this new mac save puck charger that  supports 40 gigabit per second  thunderbolt transfer speeds from a  portless iphone to a mac or ipad pro and  basically everybody who shoots prores  video will instantly want to upgrade and  then i can see apple announced that  because of it going portless this is the  first phone in the world that is  saltwater resistant and it comes with an  even bigger battery and that right there  is how i see apple introducing the  portless iphone and if you totally  disagree with me then go ahead and  comment your thoughts down below but if  you enjoyed this video click the circle  above to subscribe for more videos like  this one and check out one of those two  right over there thanks for watching and  we'll see you in the next video  [Music]

